ID: sec:doc:clinical_policy:10A:3.2.1.6
Title: 3.2.1.6 Treatment Plan (Plan of Care)
Section: 3.2.1.6
Policy: 10A
Type: clinical_policy
Effective: 2025-02-01
Citation link: [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

Content:
The treatment plan must be established once an evaluation has been administered and prior to the beginning of treatment services. The treatment plan is developed in conjunction with the medical provider, beneficiary, parent(s) or legal guardian(s) for children, and Authorized Representative for adults (if applicable). The treatment plan must consider performance in both clinical and natural environments. Treatment must be culturally appropriate. Short- and long-term functional goals and specific objectives must be determined from the evaluation. Goals and objectives must be reviewed periodically and must target functional and measurable outcomes. The treatment plan must be a specific document.
Each treatment plan in combination with the evaluation or re-evaluation written report must contain ALL the following:
duration of the therapy treatment plan consisting of the start and end date (no more than six months);
discipline specific treatment diagnosis and any related medical diagnoses;
rehabilitative or habilitative potential;
defined goals (specific and measurable goals that have reasonable expectation to be achieved within the duration of the therapy plan) for each therapeutic discipline;
skilled interventions, methodology, procedures and specific programs to be utilized;
frequency of services;
length of each treatment visit in minutes; and
name, credentials and signature of professional completing the Treatment Plan dated on or prior to the start date of the treatment plan.

[corpusSection]
ID: sec:doc:clinical_policy:3L-1:4.2.1
Title: 4.2.1 Medicaid Additional Criteria Not Covered
Section: 4.2.1
Policy: 3L-1
Type: clinical_policy
Citation link: [State Plan Personal Care Services (PCS) Provided in Congregate Settings](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/media/14986/download?attachment)

Content:
Medicaid shall not cover Personal Care Services in Congregate Settings when:
the initial independent assessment has not been completed;
the PCS is not documented as completed according to this clinical coverage policy;
a reassessment has not been completed within 30 calendar days of the end date of the previous prior authorization period because the beneficiary refused the assessment, could not be reached to schedule the assessment, or did not attend the scheduled assessment;
Note: A delayed scheduling of a reassessment due to the Comprehensive Independent Assessment Entity (CIAE) timeline must not impact the continuous coverage of PCS while the reassessment is being arranged.
the PCS is provided at a location other than the beneficiary's primary congregate living setting;
the PCS exceeds the amount approved by the Comprehensive Independent Assessment Entity (CIAE);
the PCS is not completed on the date the service is billed;
the PCS is provided prior to the effective date or after the end date of the prior authorized service period;
the PCS is performed by an individual who is the beneficiary's legally responsible person, spouse, child, parent, sibling, grandparent, grandchild, or equivalent step or in-law relationship to the beneficiary;
Clinical Coverage Policy No: 3L-1
Amended Date:
family members or other informal caregivers are willing, able, and available on a regular basis adequate to meet the beneficiary's need for personal care; or
independent medical information does not validate the assessment, PCS hours may be reduced, denied, or terminated based on the additional information.
Note
: Adult Care Home Providers are not subject to the EVV requirement.

per_question.agent_answer:
## Treatment-plan requirement

For outpatient specialized therapies, NC Medicaid requires prior approval before the provider renders services, and the treatment must be delivered under a treatment plan that meets Policy 10A’s requirements. [Policy 10A § 5.1](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) [Policy 10A, “Treatment services must adhere to the following requirements:”](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

The plan of care must be established **after the evaluation and before treatment begins**. It must be developed with the medical provider and the beneficiary; for children, it also involves the parent(s) or legal guardian(s), and for adults, an authorized representative if applicable. It must consider functioning in both clinical and natural environments and be culturally appropriate. [Policy 10A § 3.2.1.6](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

The plan must be a **specific document** and, together with the evaluation or re-evaluation report, must include:

- Start and end dates, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- The discipline-specific treatment diagnosis and related medical diagnoses;
- Rehabilitative or habilitative potential;
- Defined, specific, measurable goals for each therapy discipline that reasonably can be achieved during the plan period;
- The skilled interventions, methods, procedures, and specific programs to be used;
- Service frequency;
- Length of each visit in minutes; and
- The name, credentials, and signature of the professional completing the plan, dated on or before the plan’s start date. [Policy 10A § 3.2.1.6](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

Goals and objectives must be reviewed periodically and must target functional, measurable outcomes. The provider must review and renew or revise the treatment plan and goals **at least every six calendar months**. [Policy 10A § 3.2.1.6](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) [Policy 10A, “Treatment services must adhere to the following requirements:”](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

A verbal or written order must also be obtained before services start; the policy sets separate validity, signature, and documentation rules for those orders. [Policy 10A, “Treatment services must adhere to the following requirements:”](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

This is the current Policy 10A document, effective February 1, 2025. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

